          "If twenty pages repeat the same definition, an answer system has little reason to prefer the twenty-first. Generic content may still rank or answer a simple query, but it creates limited information gain. Citation-worthy sources usually reduce uncertainty. They publish a number that did not exist elsewhere, clarify a process using primary evidence, compare options with declared criteria, document a real implementation, maintain a reference page, or explain a specialist topic with accountable expertise.",
          "Originality does not require a national survey or an academic laboratory. A local business can analyze anonymized review themes, publish service response-time data, document a field checklist, compare maintenance approaches under defined conditions, or explain a local permitting workflow using official sources. The key is to state what the evidence represents and what it does not represent.",
